@* Sales.razor — Phase 4.2 (refactored to AdminUsers pattern)

   Customer-facing order history page at route `/Sales`. ALL authenticated
   users land here (the sidebar link in MainLayout.razor is wrapped in a
   bare <AuthorizeView>). The backend handler enforces role-based scoping:
     - Admin / Manager / Employee / ReadOnly: see ALL sales in the system
       (the "staff audit view").
     - Customer: see only their own sales (the "my orders" view).

   BACKEND CONTRACT:
     - Load: GetSalesPaginatedQuery(PageNumber, PageSize, SearchTerm?, Status?)
       → PaginatedResult<SaleListItemDto>
       - The handler resolves the current user from ICurrentUserService
         server-side, so callers CANNOT snoop on other users' sales by
         passing a foreign id.
       - We request PageSize=100 (the backend's MaxPageSize cap) so the
         grid has enough rows for client-side filtering + paging to be
         meaningful. Same approach as AdminUsers.razor.

   LAYOUT (mirrors AdminUsers.razor — "the tables should follow the shape
   and rules of the admin users page"):
     1. Page header: title + "Continue shopping" button → /Products.
        Uses the same .admin-header flex layout as AdminUsers.
     2. Sales table: RadzenDataGrid with built-in filter row per column
        (AllowFiltering=true, FilterMode=SimpleWithMenu) + built-in pager
        (AllowPaging=true, PageSize=20). No external search box, no
        external status dropdown — those were removed per the user's
        explicit request. Filtering happens per-column inside the grid,
        exactly like AdminUsers.
        Columns:
          - Sale number (text filter, clickable link to /Sales/{Id})
          - Status (dropdown filter with colored badges — Draft=gray,
            Pending=amber, Approved=blue, Invoiced=green, Cancelled=red)
          - Total (text filter on amount, right-aligned tabular-nums)
          - Customer name (staff-only, text filter)
          - Created by (staff-only audit column, text filter)
          - Created at (date filter; rendered in TEHRAN time, not UTC)
          - Actions (View icon button → /Sales/{Id})
     3. Empty state: shown only when the backend returns zero sales total
        (fresh customer). When the grid has data but the user's column
        filters match zero rows, Radzen's built-in EmptyText takes over.

   TIMEZONE (Tehran, not UTC):
     CreatedAtUtc is a DateTimeOffset in UTC. The previous implementation
     used `.LocalDateTime` which converts to the SERVER's local timezone.
     On this deployment the server runs in UTC, so users saw UTC times
     instead of Tehran times. The FormatTehranDateTime() helper now
     adds a fixed +3:30 offset inline (Tehran abolished DST in 2022,
     so a fixed offset is functionally equivalent to the IANA
     "Asia/Tehran" zone) and formats the result culture-aware. The
     same helper is used for any other UTC timestamps that may be
     added to this page in the future.

   ROLE-GATED COLUMNS:
     - Customer / non-staff users see only: Sale number, Status, Total,
       Created At, Actions. The "Customer" and "Created By" columns are
     noise for them (Customer = themselves on every row; Created By is
       an audit detail).
     - Staff (Admin/Manager/Employee/ReadOnly) see all columns including
       Customer (who the sale is for) and Created By (who keyed it in).
       Hiding these for staff would hide useful audit info; showing them
       to customers leaks nothing they don't already know but is noise.
     - Implemented via <AuthorizeView Roles="..."> around the column
       declaration — when the user isn't in those roles, the column
       (including its filter row) doesn't render at all.

   ANTIFORGERY:
     Not needed — no EditForm. All button clicks are sync navigations or
     async event handlers that dispatch queries via Wolverine. The Blazor
     circuit's anti-forgery token is established at circuit start. *@

@page "/Sales"
@attribute [Authorize]
@attribute [SuppressMessage("Usage", "BL0005:Component parameters should not be set outside their component", Justification = "Radzen exposes FilterValue/FilterOperator on RadzenDataGridColumn as the official programmatic filter API per https://radzen.com/documentation/blazor/data-grid-programmatic-filter/. The BL0005 analyzer (designed for plain Blazor component params) does not understand this Radzen-specific contract; the mutations are intentional and load-bearing.")]
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization
@using Wolverine
@using TakOne.Application.Common.Authorization
@using TakOne.Application.Sales.DTOs
@using TakOne.Application.Sales.Queries.GetSalesPaginated
@using TakOne.SharedKernel.Common
@using TakOne.SharedKernel.DTOs
@using TakOne.WebUI.Services
@inject IMessageBus MessageBus
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject IStringLocalizer<Sales> Loc
@inject ErrorDisplayService ErrorDisplay
@inject AuthenticationStateProvider AuthStateProvider
@inject ToastService Toast
@inject IJSRuntime JS

<PageTitle>@Loc["PageTitle"]</PageTitle>

<HeadContent>
    <style>
            /* Page header — mirrors AdminUsers.razor's .admin-header layout
                                                           so the two pages read as members of the same family. */
            .admin-header {
                display: flex;
                align-items: center;
                justify-content: space-between;
                gap: 1rem;
                flex-wrap: wrap;
            }

            /* Sale number — monospace + primary color so it reads as a clickable
                                                           identifier. The link itself is the clickable element; the wrapper
                                                           just provides consistent typography. */
            .sale-number-link {
                font-family: var(--rz-monospace-font-family, ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace);
                font-weight: 600;
                color: var(--rz-primary);
                text-decoration: none;
            }

                .sale-number-link:hover {
                    text-decoration: underline;
                }

            /* Status badge — RadzenBadge with BadgeStyle handles colors; this
                                                           just normalizes the width so badges in a column line up. */
            .sale-status-badge {
                min-width: 96px;
                display: inline-flex;
                justify-content: center;
            }

            /* Total — right-aligned tabular numbers so columns line up. */
            .sale-total {
                font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
                font-weight: 600;
            }

            /* Muted timestamp for the "Created At" column. The timestamp is
                                                           secondary info; the sale number is the primary identifier. */
            .sale-timestamp {
                color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color);
                font-size: 0.9rem;
            }

            .row-actions {
                display: flex;
                gap: 0.25rem;
                justify-content: center;
                align-items: center;
            }

            /* ── Date-range filter toolbar (Round 3) ───────────────────
               A single horizontal row: calendar icon, From picker, an
               em-dash separator, To picker, Apply button, and — when a
               range is active — a chip restating the applied range with
               a built-in clear (×) button. Wraps on narrow screens. */
            .sales-datefilter {
                display: flex;
                align-items: center;
                gap: 0.6rem;
                flex-wrap: wrap;
            }

            .sales-datefilter-sep {
                color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color);
                user-select: none;
            }

            .sales-datefilter-chip {
                display: inline-flex;
                align-items: center;
                gap: 0.4rem;
                padding: 0.28rem 0.7rem;
                border-radius: 999px;
                background: var(--rz-secondary-background-color, var(--rz-base-background-color));
                border: 1px solid var(--rz-border-color);
                color: var(--rz-primary);
                font-size: 0.8rem;
                font-weight: 600;
                font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
            }

            .sales-datefilter-chip-x {
                /* Native button reset — the RadzenIcon inside provides the ×. */
                display: inline-flex;
                align-items: center;
                justify-content: center;
                padding: 0.05rem;
                border: none;
                background: transparent;
                color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color);
                cursor: pointer;
                border-radius: 50%;
                min-height: 44px; /* a11y: comfortable touch target */
                min-width: 44px;
            }

            .sales-datefilter-chip-x:hover {
                color: var(--rz-danger, #e74c3c);
                background: var(--rz-secondary-background-color, transparent);
            }
    </style>
</HeadContent>

<RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Vertical" Gap="1rem">
    @* ── Page header ──
       Title + "Continue shopping" button. Uses the same .admin-header
       flex layout as AdminUsers.razor so the two pages share a common
       header shape (per the user's request that this page follow the
       admin users page's rules). The button is always visible — any
       user browsing their orders might want to go back to shopping. *@
    <div class="admin-header">
        <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="0.75rem">
            <RadzenIcon Icon="receipt_long" Style="font-size: 1.75rem; color: var(--rz-primary);" />
            <RadzenText Text="@Loc["PageHeading"]" TextStyle="TextStyle.H5" Style="font-weight: 600; margin: 0;" />
        </RadzenStack>

        <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["ContinueShoppingButton"]"
                      Icon="storefront"
                      ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                      Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                      Click="@(() => Navigation.NavigateTo("/Products", forceLoad: false))" />
    </div>

    @* ── Loading skeleton (Round 3 styling) ──
       Same 7-column rhythm as the real grid so the layout doesn't jump
       when data replaces the skeleton. Uses the shared .tm-skel utilities
       from app.css (theme-adaptive, prefers-reduced-motion aware). *@
    @if (_isLoading)
    {
        <RadzenCard Variant="Variant.Filled" Class="sales-grid" Style="padding: 0.5rem;">
            <div class="tm-skel-stack" aria-busy="true" aria-live="polite" style="padding: 0.5rem;">
                @for (var i = 0; i < 8; i++)
                {
                    <div class="tm-skel-grid" style="--tm-skel-cols: 1.3fr 0.9fr 0.8fr 1fr 1fr 1.1fr 0.7fr;">
                        <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--line tm-skel--w80"></div>
                        <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--chip"></div>
                        <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--line"></div>
                        <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--line tm-skel--w60"></div>
                        <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--line tm-skel--w60"></div>
                        <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--line tm-skel--w80"></div>
                        <div class="tm-skel tm-skel--chip"></div>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>
        </RadzenCard>
    }

    @* ── Error banner ── *@
    else if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(_loadError))
    {
        <RadzenAlert Title="@Loc["ErrorTitle"]"
                     Text="@_loadError"
                     AlertStyle="AlertStyle.Danger"
                     Variant="Variant.Filled"
                     ShowIcon="true">
            <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["RetryButton"]"
                          Icon="refresh"
                          ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Primary"
                          Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                          Click="@LoadSalesAsync"
                          Style="margin-top: 0.5rem;" />
        </RadzenAlert>
    }

    @* ── Empty state (no orders at all, or none in the applied range) ──
       When NO date filter is applied and the backend returns zero sales
       total, this is a fresh customer with no orders yet — the original
       "start shopping" empty state. When a date range IS applied and
       matches zero rows, the user just filtered themselves into an empty
       corner — a targeted message (plus the Clear button in the toolbar
       above) beats telling them to go shopping. When the grid has data
       but the user's column filters match zero rows, Radzen's built-in
       EmptyText takes over inside the grid. *@
    else if (_sales.Count == 0)
    {
        @if (HasAppliedDateFilter)
        {
            <RadzenCard Variant="Variant.Filled" Style="padding: 3rem; text-align: center;">
                <RadzenStack AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="1rem">
                    <RadzenIcon Icon="event_busy"
                                Style="font-size: 3rem; color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color);" />
                    <RadzenText Text="@Loc["EmptyTitleFiltered"]"
                                TextStyle="TextStyle.H6" />
                    <RadzenText Text="@Loc["EmptyMessageFiltered"]"
                                Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); max-width: 360px;" />
                    <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["ClearDateFilterButton"]"
                                  Icon="filter_alt_off"
                                  ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                                  Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                                  Click="@ClearDateFilterAsync" />
                </RadzenStack>
            </RadzenCard>
        }
        else
        {
            <RadzenCard Variant="Variant.Filled" Style="padding: 3rem; text-align: center;">
                <RadzenStack AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Gap="1rem">
                    <RadzenIcon Icon="receipt_long"
                                Style="font-size: 3rem; color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color);" />
                    <RadzenText Text="@Loc["EmptyTitle"]"
                                TextStyle="TextStyle.H6" />
                    <RadzenText Text="@Loc["EmptyMessage"]"
                                Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); max-width: 360px;" />
                    <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal" Gap="0.5rem" Wrap="FlexWrap.Wrap" JustifyContent="JustifyContent.Center">
                        <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["GoToCartButton"]"
                                      Icon="shopping_cart"
                                      ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Primary"
                                      Click="@(() => Navigation.NavigateTo("/Cart", forceLoad: false))" />
                        <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["BrowseProductsButton"]"
                                      Icon="inventory_2"
                                      ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                                      Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                                      Click="@(() => Navigation.NavigateTo("/Products", forceLoad: false))" />
                    </RadzenStack>
                </RadzenStack>
            </RadzenCard>
        }
    }

    @* ── Date-range filter toolbar (Round 3) ──
       Server-side creation-date filter: the picked Tehran-local dates are
       converted to UTC half-open interval instants [from, to) and pushed
       into GetSalesPaginatedQuery, so the SQL WHERE clause + TotalCount
       are accurate (the grid's per-column filters stay client-side on
       the loaded page — same split as before). Rendered above the grid
       whenever there is data to filter; the filtered-empty state carries
       its own one-click Clear instead. The active range renders as a
       chip so the user can always see WHY the list is what it is. *@

    @* ── Sales table ──
       RadzenDataGrid with built-in filter row per column (AllowFiltering +
       FilterMode.SimpleWithMenu) + built-in pager (AllowPaging, PageSize=20).
       Mirrors the AdminUsers.razor grid — no external search box, no
       external status dropdown. Each column has its own filter input in a
       dedicated filter row beneath the header:
         - Sale Number → text filter (Radzen default)
         - Status      → dropdown with colored badges
         - Total       → text filter (Radzen default, on Total.Amount)
         - Customer    → text filter (Radzen default, staff-only column)
         - Created By  → text filter (Radzen default, staff-only column)
         - Created At  → date filter (Radzen default)
         - Actions     → no filter (Filterable=false)
       Data is loaded once (PageSize=100, the backend cap) and filtered +
       paged client-side — same approach as AdminUsers.razor. *@
    else
    {
        <RadzenCard Variant="Variant.Filled" Style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <div class="sales-datefilter" role="group" aria-label="@Loc["FilterAriaLabel"]">
                <RadzenIcon Icon="calendar_month"
                            Style="color: var(--rz-primary); align-self: center;" />
                <RadzenDatePicker @bind-Value="_dateFrom"
                                  TValue="DateTime?"
                                  ShowTime="false"
                                  AllowClear="true"
                                  Placeholder="@Loc["FilterDateFrom"]"
                                  DateFormat="yyyy/MM/dd"
                                  ShowWeekNumbers="false"
                                  Style="width: 10.5rem;"
                                  Disabled="@_isLoading" />
                <span class="sales-datefilter-sep" aria-hidden="true">—</span>
                <RadzenDatePicker @bind-Value="_dateTo"
                                  TValue="DateTime?"
                                  ShowTime="false"
                                  AllowClear="true"
                                  Placeholder="@Loc["FilterDateTo"]"
                                  DateFormat="yyyy/MM/dd"
                                  ShowWeekNumbers="false"
                                  Style="width: 10.5rem;"
                                  Disabled="@_isLoading" />
                <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["ApplyDateFilterButton"]"
                              Icon="filter_alt"
                              ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Primary"
                              Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                              Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                              Disabled="@(_isLoading || (_dateFrom is null && _dateTo is null))"
                              Click="@ApplyDateFilterAsync" />
                @if (HasAppliedDateFilter)
                {
                    <span class="sales-datefilter-chip">
                        <RadzenIcon Icon="event_available" />
                        <span>@FormatAppliedRange()</span>
                        <button type="button"
                                class="sales-datefilter-chip-x"
                                title="@Loc["ClearDateFilterButton"]"
                                aria-label="@Loc["ClearDateFilterButton"]"
                                @onclick="@ClearDateFilterAsync">
                            <RadzenIcon Icon="close" />
                        </button>
                    </span>
                }

                @* Export CSV (Round 3) — exports exactly what the list shows
                   (the loaded page, including the applied date range and the
                   server-side scoping: a customer exports their own sales,
                   staff export the audit view). "What you see is what you
                   get" beats a surprise full-table dump. Spacer pushes it to
                   the trailing edge on wide screens. *@
                <span style="flex: 1;"></span>
                <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["ExportCsvButton"]"
                              Icon="download"
                              ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Secondary"
                              Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                              Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                              Disabled="@(_isLoading || _sales.Count == 0)"
                              Click="@ExportSalesCsvAsync" />
            </div>
        </RadzenCard>

        <RadzenCard Variant="Variant.Filled" Class="sales-grid" Style="padding: 0.5rem;">
            <RadzenDataGrid @ref="grid"
                            Data="@_sales"
                            TItem="SaleListItemDto"
                            AllowSorting="true"
                            AllowFiltering="true"
                            FilterMode="FilterMode.SimpleWithMenu"
                            FilterCaseSensitivity="FilterCaseSensitivity.CaseInsensitive"
                            AllowPaging="true"
                            PageSize="20"
                            PagerHorizontalAlign="HorizontalAlign.Center"
                            EmptyText="@Loc["EmptyText"]"
                            AllowColumnReorder="false"
                            Style="width: 100%;">
                <Columns>

                    @* ── Sale number — text filter (Radzen default) ──
                       Clickable link to /Sales/{Id}. Monospace + primary
                       color so it reads as an identifier.

                       B2 deferred-allocation: drafts have no permanent
                       SaleNumber, so the column binds to DisplayNumber
                       (real SaleNumber for submitted sales, or
                       DRAFT-{Guid[0..8]} for drafts). This makes drafts
                       searchable — typing "DRAFT" or the Guid prefix in
                       the filter row matches draft rows. *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="SaleListItemDto"
                                          Property="DisplayNumber"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColSaleNumber"]"
                                          Width="240px">
                        <Template Context="sale">
                            <RadzenLink Text="@(CultureFormat.FormatDigits(sale.DisplayNumber))"
                                        Path="@($"/Sales/{sale.Id}")"
                                        class="sale-number-link"
                                        title="@(GetSaleNumberTooltip(sale))" />
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Status — dropdown filter with colored tags ──
                       Mirrors AdminUsers.razor's Status column. The dropdown
                       uses string keys ("Draft"/"Pending"/"Approved"/
                       "Invoiced"/"Cancelled") which match the SaleListItemDto.Status
                       property exactly. AllowClear resets to null (All statuses).
                       The dropdown's ValueTemplate shows the selected value as a
                       colored badge; Template shows each option as a colored badge.
                       Badge colors: Draft=gray(Base), Pending=amber(Warning),
                       Approved=blue(Info), Invoiced=green(Success),
                       Cancelled=red(Danger). Filter is client-side via
                       column.FilterValue + grid.Reload(). *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="SaleListItemDto"
                                          Property="Status"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColStatus"]"
                                          Width="140px"
                                          TextAlign="TextAlign.Center"
                                          FilterOperator="FilterOperator.Equals">
                        <Template Context="sale">
                            <RadzenBadge Text="@GetStatusLabel(sale.Status)"
                                         BadgeStyle="@GetStatusBadgeStyle(sale.Status)"
                                         IsPill="true"
                                         class="sale-status-badge" />
                        </Template>
                        <FilterTemplate Context="column">
                            <RadzenDropDown @bind-Value="_selectedStatusFilter"
                                            Data="@_statusFilterOptions"
                                            TValue="string"
                                            AllowClear="true"
                                            Placeholder="@Loc["StatusAll"]"
                                            Change="@(args => OnStatusFilterChanged(_selectedStatusFilter, column))"
                                            Style="width: 100%; min-width: 130px;">
                                <ValueTemplate Context="selected">
                                    @{
                                        var selectedStr = selected?.ToString();
                                    }
                                    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(selectedStr))
                                    {
                                        <RadzenBadge Text="@GetStatusLabel(selectedStr)"
                                                     BadgeStyle="@GetStatusBadgeStyle(selectedStr)"
                                                     IsPill="true" />
                                    }
                                    else
                                    {
                                        <span>@Loc["StatusAll"]</span>
                                    }
                                </ValueTemplate>
                                <Template Context="statusOption">
                                    @{
                                        var statusStr = statusOption?.ToString();
                                    }
                                    <RadzenBadge Text="@GetStatusLabel(statusStr)"
                                                 BadgeStyle="@GetStatusBadgeStyle(statusStr)"
                                                 IsPill="true" />
                                </Template>
                            </RadzenDropDown>
                        </FilterTemplate>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Total — text filter on Total.Amount ──
                       Right-aligned tabular-numeric. The filter operates on
                       the underlying decimal amount (not the formatted
                       string), so typing "15000" matches sales whose total
                       amount is 15000 regardless of currency. *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="SaleListItemDto"
                                          Property="Total.Amount"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColTotal"]"
                                          Width="140px"
                                          TextAlign="TextAlign.Right">
                        <Template Context="sale">
                            <span class="sale-total">@FormatMoney(sale.Total)</span>
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Customer name — staff-only, text filter ──
                       For a customer browsing their own orders this column
                       would be themselves on every row, which is noise.
                       Hiding it for non-staff keeps the table compact on
                       mobile.

                       IMPORTANT — WHY Visible= (not <AuthorizeView>):
                       Earlier these two columns were wrapped in
                       <AuthorizeView Roles="...">. That worked for
                       visibility, but it broke column ORDER: Blazor's
                       AuthorizeView is a component (not a directive), so
                       when RadzenDataGrid walks its <Columns> RenderFragment
                       to collect columns, the ones nested inside an
                       AuthorizeView's child content get appended to the
                       grid's column list AFTER all the unwrapped columns.
                       The runtime order then became
                       SaleNumber, Status, Total, CreatedAt, Actions,
                       Customer, CreatedBy — i.e. Created By appeared AFTER
                       Actions, which was wrong. Switching to Visible="@_isStaff"
                       keeps the columns in source order (Actions last) AND
                       preserves role-gating, because Visible is a parameter
                       on the column itself rather than a wrapper component
                       that re-orders the RenderFragment. *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="SaleListItemDto"
                                          Property="CustomerName"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColCustomer"]"
                                          Width="*"
                                          Visible="@_isStaff">
                        <Template Context="sale">
                            <RadzenText Text="@sale.CustomerName" Style="font-weight: 500;" />
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Created by — staff-only audit column, text filter ──
                       Shows which staff member keyed the sale in (often the
                       customer themselves for self-service sales, but could
                       be an employee acting on behalf of a customer).
                       See the Customer column's comment for why we use
                       Visible=@_isStaff instead of <AuthorizeView>. *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="SaleListItemDto"
                                          Property="CreatedByName"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColCreatedBy"]"
                                          Width="*"
                                          Visible="@_isStaff">
                        <Template Context="sale">
                            <RadzenText Text="@sale.CreatedByName"
                                        Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color);" />
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Created at — Tehran time, date filter ──
                       The underlying Property is CreatedAtUtc (DateTimeOffset).
                       The filter operates on the UTC value (so it's timezone-
                       stable across deployments), but the cell Template
                       renders the value converted to Tehran time via
                       FormatTehranDateTime() — see the helper's doc comment
                       for why .LocalDateTime is wrong here. *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="SaleListItemDto"
                                          Property="CreatedAtUtc"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColCreated"]"
                                          Width="180px">
                        <Template Context="sale">
                            <span class="sale-timestamp">@FormatTehranDateTime(sale.CreatedAtUtc)</span>
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>

                    @* ── Actions — View icon button, no filter ──
                       Same target as the sale number link, but provides a
                       clearer touch target on mobile. Matches AdminUsers'
                       action button styling (icon-only text button with
                       title attribute). *@
                    <RadzenDataGridColumn TItem="SaleListItemDto"
                                          Title="@Loc["ColActions"]"
                                          Width="80px"
                                          TextAlign="TextAlign.Center"
                                          Sortable="false"
                                          Filterable="false">
                        <Template Context="sale">
                            <div class="row-actions">
                                <RadzenButton Icon="visibility"
                                              Variant="Variant.Text"
                                              ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Info"
                                              title="@Loc["ActionViewDetail"]"
                                              aria-label="@Loc["ActionViewDetail"]"
                                              Click="@(() => GoToSaleDetail(sale.Id))" />
                            </div>
                        </Template>
                    </RadzenDataGridColumn>
                </Columns>
            </RadzenDataGrid>
        </RadzenCard>
    }
</RadzenStack>

@code {
    // ── State ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// The full list of sales returned by the backend. We request
    /// PageSize=100 (the backend cap) so the grid has enough rows for
    /// client-side filtering + paging. Same approach as AdminUsers.razor.
    /// </summary>
    private List<SaleListItemDto> _sales = new();

    /// <summary>
    /// Grid reference — needed so the status filter's Change handler can
    /// call grid.Reload() after setting column.FilterValue.
    /// </summary>
    private RadzenDataGrid<SaleListItemDto> grid = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// Currently-selected status filter key ("Draft"/"Pending"/"Approved"/
    /// "Invoiced"/"Cancelled"), or null when the filter is cleared
    /// (AllowClear=true passes null on clear).
    /// </summary>
    private string? _selectedStatusFilter;

    /// <summary>
    /// Status filter dropdown options — string keys matching the SaleStatus
    /// enum's ToString() output. The dropdown's ValueTemplate + Template
    /// render each key as a localized colored badge via GetStatusLabel() +
    /// GetStatusBadgeStyle(), so the user sees friendly localized text
    /// while the underlying filter value stays culture-neutral.
    /// </summary>
    private readonly List<string> _statusFilterOptions = new()
    {
        "Draft", "Pending", "Approved", "Invoiced", "Cancelled"
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// Loading/error state.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _isLoading = true;
    private string _loadError = string.Empty;

    // ── Date-range filter (Round 3) ──────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// The picked (not yet applied) From date, as a Tehran-LOCAL date —
    /// what the RadzenDatePicker binds to. Local midnight semantics: the
    /// sale list renders CreatedAtUtc in Tehran time, so "from Aug 1"
    /// must mean "from Aug 1 00:00 Tehran", not "from Aug 1 00:00 UTC".
    /// </summary>
    private DateTime? _dateFrom;

    /// <summary>
    /// The picked (not yet applied) To date, Tehran-local. Applied as an
    /// EXCLUSIVE upper bound at local midnight of the NEXT day — "through
    /// Aug 29" includes every sale placed any time on Aug 29.
    /// </summary>
    private DateTime? _dateTo;

    /// <summary>
    /// The APPLIED range as UTC half-open interval instants — what
    /// LoadSalesAsync actually dispatches. Kept separate from the picker
    /// fields so the list always reflects the last APPLIED filter (the
    /// pickers can hold an unapplied edit while the grid shows the old
    /// range — standard ERP apply-on-click semantics).
    /// </summary>
    private DateTime? _appliedFromUtc;
    private DateTime? _appliedToUtc;

    /// <summary>
    /// True when a server-side date range is currently applied — drives
    /// the chip rendering and the filtered-empty state's copy.
    /// </summary>
    private bool HasAppliedDateFilter => _appliedFromUtc.HasValue || _appliedToUtc.HasValue;

    /// <summary>
    /// Tehran's fixed UTC offset. Iran abolished DST in 2022, so +03:30
    /// is exact for every date the ERP will ever render (see the class
    /// docs under "Tehran timezone" below for why we don't use
    /// TimeZoneInfo).
    /// </summary>
    private static readonly TimeSpan TehranUtcOffset = TimeSpan.FromHours(3.5);

    /// <summary>
    /// Converts a Tehran-local DATE to the UTC instant of its local
    /// midnight: <c>localDate − 03:30</c>. Used for both bounds — the To
    /// bound gets <c>.AddDays(1)</c> first (exclusive upper at the NEXT
    /// day's local midnight).
    /// </summary>
    private static DateTime ToUtcInstant(DateTime localDate)
        => DateTime.SpecifyKind(localDate.Date, DateTimeKind.Utc) - TehranUtcOffset;

    /// <summary>
    /// True if the current user is in Admin / Manager / Employee / ReadOnly
    /// role (i.e. "staff" — sees all sales + the Customer and Created By
    /// audit columns). Customers (and any non-staff role) see only their own
    /// sales and a narrower column set.
    ///
    /// WHY THIS EXISTS (and why we don't use &lt;AuthorizeView&gt; for the
    /// staff-only columns anymore):
    ///   The Customer and Created By columns used to be wrapped in
    ///   &lt;AuthorizeView Roles="..."&gt;. That worked for visibility, but
    ///   it broke column ORDER. AuthorizeView is a Blazor COMPONENT (not a
    ///   directive like @if), so when RadzenDataGrid walks its &lt;Columns&gt;
    ///   RenderFragment to collect column instances, the columns nested
    ///   inside an AuthorizeView's child content are added to the grid's
    ///   column list AFTER all the unwrapped columns. The runtime order
    ///   became SaleNumber, Status, Total, CreatedAt, Actions, Customer,
    ///   CreatedBy — i.e. Created By appeared AFTER Actions, which was
    ///   wrong.
    ///   Switching to Visible="@_isStaff" on the column itself fixes this
    ///   because Visible is a parameter on the column (which is already in
    ///   the grid's column collection at the right position), not a wrapper
    ///   component that re-orders the RenderFragment.
    ///
    /// WHY AuthStateProvider (not ICurrentUserService):
    ///   Same reason as SaleDetail.razor — ICurrentUserService resolves
    ///   from IHttpContextAccessor.HttpContext, which is only populated
    ///   during the initial prerender request and is null over the SignalR
    ///   circuit. AuthStateProvider is the canonical source of role info
    ///   for interactive Blazor Server components.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _isStaff;

    // ── Tehran timezone ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Tehran abolished DST in 2022, so a fixed UTC+03:30 offset is
    // functionally equivalent to the IANA "Asia/Tehran" zone for all
    // dates from 2022 onward. We do the offset arithmetic inline in
    // FormatTehranDateTime instead of using a static TimeZoneInfo field
    // — this avoids (a) platform-specific timezone-lookup failures
    // that were causing the field to be null on some Windows deployments,
    // and (b) Blazor Server hot-reload limitations where static field
    // initializers don't get re-evaluated after a code change.

    // ── Lifecycle ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Resolve _isStaff synchronously before the first render so the
        // Customer and Created By columns don't flicker (Visible is bound
        // to _isStaff; if we set it after the first render, the grid would
        // briefly show all 7 columns to a customer before collapsing to 5).
        var authState = await AuthStateProvider.GetAuthenticationStateAsync();
        var user = authState.User;
        _isStaff = user.IsInRole(Roles.Admin)
                   || user.IsInRole(Roles.Manager)
                   || user.IsInRole(Roles.Employee)
                   || user.IsInRole(Roles.ReadOnly);

        try
        {
            await LoadSalesAsync();
        }
        finally
        {
            _isLoading = false;
        }
    }

    // ── Data loading ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    private async Task LoadSalesAsync()
    {
        _isLoading = true;
        _loadError = string.Empty;

        try
        {
            // Load up to 100 sales (the backend cap) so the grid has
            // enough rows for client-side filtering + paging. Same
            // approach as AdminUsers.razor — backend caps PageSize at
            // MaxPageSize=100, so anything larger is silently clamped.
            //
            // SearchTerm + Status are null: filtering happens client-side
            // via Radzen's per-column filter row (no external filter bar).
            // The DATE RANGE is the one SERVER-side filter — it must prune
            // rows BEFORE the PageSize=100 slice, otherwise "show me last
            // week" on a 10,000-sale DB returns the newest 100 regardless
            // of the range (the pre-Round-3 behavior — everything past row
            // 100 was invisible).
            var query = new GetSalesPaginatedQuery
            {
                PageNumber = 1,
                PageSize = 100,
                SearchTerm = null,
                Status = null,
                FromDateUtc = _appliedFromUtc,
                ToDateUtc = _appliedToUtc
            };

            var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<PaginatedResult<SaleListItemDto>>(query);
            _sales = result.Items.ToList();
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            _loadError = ErrorDisplay.Unexpected(UnexpectedErrorCodes.Sales_LoadFailure);
        }
        finally
        {
            _isLoading = false;
        }
    }

    // ── Date-range filter handlers ─────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Applies the picked range: converts the local dates to the UTC
    /// half-open interval [from-local-midnight, to+1day-local-midnight),
    /// stores them as the APPLIED bounds, and reloads. A degenerate range
    /// (From AFTER To) is rejected with a toast BEFORE reloading — clearer
    /// than silently showing an empty list the user would misread as "no
    /// orders in that period".
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ApplyDateFilterAsync()
    {
        if (_dateFrom.HasValue && _dateTo.HasValue && _dateFrom.Value.Date > _dateTo.Value.Date)
        {
            await Toast.Warning(Loc["DateFilterInvalidRange"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
            return;
        }

        _appliedFromUtc = _dateFrom.HasValue ? ToUtcInstant(_dateFrom.Value) : null;
        _appliedToUtc = _dateTo.HasValue ? ToUtcInstant(_dateTo.Value.AddDays(1)) : null;

        await LoadSalesAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Clears BOTH the pickers and the applied range, then reloads the
    /// unfiltered list. Called from the chip's ×, the filtered-empty
    /// state's Clear button, and the toolbar.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ClearDateFilterAsync()
    {
        _dateFrom = null;
        _dateTo = null;
        _appliedFromUtc = null;
        _appliedToUtc = null;

        await LoadSalesAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Human-readable restatement of the applied range for the chip —
    /// localized dates via the page's CultureFormat helper (Persian
    /// digits + Jalali calendar in fa-IR), and an open-ended ellipsis
    /// when only one bound is set.
    /// </summary>
    private string FormatAppliedRange()
    {
        // Display in Tehran local dates (what the user picked), converting
        // the stored UTC instants back: local = utc + 03:30.
        var fromLocal = _appliedFromUtc.HasValue
            ? (_appliedFromUtc.Value + TehranUtcOffset).ToString("yyyy/MM/dd", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
            : "…";
        var toLocal = _appliedToUtc.HasValue
            ? ((_appliedToUtc.Value + TehranUtcOffset).AddDays(-1)).ToString("yyyy/MM/dd", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)
            : "…";

        return $"{CultureFormat.ToCultureDigits(fromLocal)} – {CultureFormat.ToCultureDigits(toLocal)}";
    }

    // ── CSV export (Round 3) ────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Builds a CSV of the CURRENTLY LOADED sales (the same rows the grid
    /// shows — server-side date range + role scoping included; the grid's
    /// per-column client-side filters are NOT applied, matching the
    /// "export what the server returned" contract documented on the
    /// button) and downloads it via the takDownload.js helper.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// <b>FORMAT</b> (see CsvBuilder's class docs): RFC 4180 quoting,
    /// CRLF records, invariant number formatting, ISO-8601 timestamps,
    /// UTF-8 + BOM at save time (Excel-safe Persian text). Money exports
    /// the RAW amount + currency code — data-processing friendly.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <b>LOCALIZED HEADERS + STATUS</b>: the header captions and the
    /// status labels use the page's resx (same wording as the grid), so
    /// an fa-IR user gets a Persian-headed spreadsheet.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task ExportSalesCsvAsync()
    {
        if (_sales.Count == 0)
        {
            return; // the button is disabled for empty lists — belt + braces
        }

        try
        {
            // Column shape mirrors the grid: number, status, total (+currency
            // as its own column for data processing), staff-only customer +
            // creator audit columns, and the creation timestamp.
            var header = new List<string>
            {
                Loc["ColSaleNumber"], Loc["ColStatus"], Loc["ColTotal"], Loc["ColCurrency"]
            };
            if (_isStaff)
            {
                header.Add(Loc["ColCustomer"]);
                header.Add(Loc["ColCreatedBy"]);
            }
            header.Add(Loc["ColCreatedAt"]);

            var builder = new CsvBuilder();
            builder.AddHeader(header.ToArray());

            foreach (var sale in _sales)
            {
                var row = new List<object?>
                {
                    sale.SaleNumber ?? $"DRAFT-{sale.Id.ToString()[..8].ToUpperInvariant()}",
                    GetStatusLabel(sale.Status),
                    sale.Total?.Amount,
                    sale.Total?.Currency,
                };
                if (_isStaff)
                {
                    row.Add(sale.CustomerName);
                    row.Add(sale.CreatedByName);
                }
                row.Add(sale.CreatedAtUtc);

                builder.AddRow(row.ToArray());
            }

            var csv = builder.ToString();

            var fileName = $"takone-sales-{DateTime.UtcNow:yyyyMMdd-HHmmss}.csv";
            await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("takDownload.csv", fileName, csv);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // JS interop can fail after a circuit reconnect — a visible
            // toast beats a silently dead button.
            await Toast.Warning(Loc["ExportCsvError"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
        }
    }

    // ── Filter handlers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // Each handler sets column.FilterValue and calls grid.Reload() to
    // trigger Radzen's client-side filtering. The bound field is updated
    // by @bind-Value before the Change event fires, so we read the field
    // (not the args parameter) inside the handler.

    private async Task OnStatusFilterChanged(string? value, RadzenDataGridColumn<SaleListItemDto> column)
    {
        _selectedStatusFilter = value;

        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(value))
        {
            column.FilterValue = null;
        }
        else
        {
            // Status is a string property on SaleListItemDto, so we can
            // pass the culture-neutral key directly as the filter value.
            column.FilterValue = value;
            column.FilterOperator = FilterOperator.Equals;
        }

        await grid.Reload();
    }

    // ── Navigation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    private void GoToSaleDetail(Guid saleId)
    {
        Navigation.NavigateTo($"/Sales/{saleId}", forceLoad: false);
    }

    // ── Display helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Returns a tooltip for the sale-number link. When the sale is a Draft
    /// (no permanent SaleNumber), returns a localized explanation of the
    /// pseudo-id. Returns null for submitted sales so the browser doesn't
    /// render an empty title attribute.
    /// </summary>
    private string? GetSaleNumberTooltip(SaleListItemDto sale) =>
        sale.SaleNumber is null ? (string?)Loc["DraftPseudoIdTooltip"] : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// Formats a MoneyDto for display. IRR is labelled "ریال" (no division —
    /// order history shows the real Rial amount on record). Other currencies
    /// pass through unchanged.
    /// Delegates to <see cref="CultureFormat.FormatMoneyRial"/> for
    /// culture-native digits (Persian digits in fa-IR mode, ASCII in en-US).
    /// </summary>
    private static string FormatMoney(MoneyDto? money)
        => CultureFormat.FormatMoneyRial(money);

    /// <summary>
    /// Formats a UTC timestamp for display in Tehran time, culture-aware.
    /// Delegates to <see cref="CultureFormat.FormatTehranDateTime(DateTimeOffset)"/>,
    /// which handles the Tehran offset (+3:30, no DST since 2022), the
    /// Jalali calendar conversion in fa-IR mode, and the Persian digit
    /// swap. The previous implementation called
    /// <c>tehran.DateTime.ToString("g", CultureInfo.CurrentCulture)</c>
    /// directly, which produced ASCII digits in fa-IR mode (because .NET's
    /// NumberFormatInfo.NativeDigits is informational-only) and a
    /// Gregorian year (because .NET's fa-IR culture's default calendar is
    /// Gregorian, not Jalali). The centralized helper fixes both issues.
    /// </summary>
    private static string FormatTehranDateTime(DateTimeOffset utc)
        => CultureFormat.FormatTehranDateTime(utc);

    /// <summary>
    /// Maps a sale status string (the SaleStatus enum's ToString() output)
    /// to a Radzen BadgeStyle for color coding:
    ///   - Draft     → Base      (gray, "in progress, not yet submitted")
    ///   - Pending   → Warning   (amber, "awaiting approval")
    ///   - Approved  → Info      (blue, "approved, ready to invoice")
    ///   - Invoiced  → Success   (green, "complete")
    ///   - Cancelled → Danger    (red, "cancelled")
    ///
    /// Used by both the cell Template (string overload — sale.Status) and
    /// the filter dropdown's ValueTemplate/Template (string overload —
    /// "Draft"/"Pending"/etc. keys).
    ///
    /// Unknown status strings (defensive: shouldn't happen unless the enum
    /// gains a new value without updating this mapping) fall back to Base.
    /// </summary>
    private static BadgeStyle GetStatusBadgeStyle(string? status) => status switch
    {
        "Draft" => BadgeStyle.Base,
        "Pending" => BadgeStyle.Warning,
        "Approved" => BadgeStyle.Info,
        "Invoiced" => BadgeStyle.Success,
        "Cancelled" => BadgeStyle.Danger,
        _ => BadgeStyle.Base
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// Returns a localized label for the sale status. Falls back to the
    /// raw status string if no localization key matches (defensive —
    /// shouldn't happen unless the enum gains a new value).
    /// </summary>
    private string GetStatusLabel(string? status)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(status)) return Loc["StatusAll"];
        var key = $"Status{status}";
        var localized = Loc[key];
        // If the localizer can't find the key, it returns the key itself.
        // In that case, fall back to the raw status string.
        return localized == key ? status : localized;
    }
}